// Stoic quotes for the new tab. Wording follows common public-domain or widely quoted translations.
const stoicQuotes = [
  // Marcus Aurelius
  { text: "You have power over your mind, not outside events. Realize this, and you will find strength.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The happiness of your life depends upon the quality of your thoughts.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Waste no more time arguing about what a good man should be. Be one.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The best revenge is to be unlike him who performed the injury.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Very little is needed to make a happy life; it is all within yourself, in your way of thinking.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The impediment to action advances action. What stands in the way becomes the way.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "If it is not right, do not do it; if it is not true, do not say it.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Loss is nothing else but change, and change is Nature's delight.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Do every act of your life as though it were the very last act of your life.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Accept the things to which fate binds you, and love the people with whom fate brings you together, but do so with all your heart.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Dwell on the beauty of life. Watch the stars, and see yourself running with them.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The universe is change; our life is what our thoughts make it.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "How much more grievous are the consequences of anger than the causes of it.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Never esteem anything as of advantage to you that will make you break your word or lose your self-respect.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Look back over the past, with its changing empires that rose and fell, and you can foresee the future too.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Time is a sort of river of passing events, and strong is its current; no sooner is a thing brought to sight than it is swept by and another takes its place.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Confine yourself to the present.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Do not act as if you were going to live ten thousand years. Death hangs over you. While you live, while it is in your power, be good.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The things you think about determine the quality of your mind. Your soul takes on the color of your thoughts.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Choose not to be harmed, and you won't feel harmed. Don't feel harmed, and you haven't been.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "If you are distressed by anything external, the pain is not due to the thing itself, but to your estimate of it; and this you have the power to revoke at any moment.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Nowhere can man find a quieter or more untroubled retreat than in his own soul.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Whatever happens at all happens as it should.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Everything is only for a day, both that which remembers and that which is remembered.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Soon you will have forgotten all things; soon all things will have forgotten you.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Think of yourself as dead. You have lived your life. Now take what's left and live it properly.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "When you wake up in the morning, tell yourself: the people I deal with today will be meddling, ungrateful, arrogant, dishonest, jealous and surly.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "At dawn, when you have trouble getting out of bed, tell yourself: I have to go to work, as a human being.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The first rule is to keep an untroubled spirit. The second is to look things in the face and know them for what they are.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Be like the cliff against which the waves continually break; but it stands firm and tames the fury of the water around it.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Never let the future disturb you. You will meet it, if you have to, with the same weapons of reason which today arm you against the present.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Look within. Within is the fountain of good, and it will ever bubble up, if thou wilt ever dig.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Remember that man lives only in the present, in this fleeting instant; all the rest of his life is either past and gone, or not yet revealed.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The art of living is more like wrestling than dancing.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "A man's worth is no greater than the worth of his ambitions.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "How ridiculous and how strange to be surprised at anything which happens in life.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Whenever you are about to find fault with someone, ask yourself: what fault of mine most nearly resembles the one I am about to criticize?", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Kindness is invincible, but only when it's sincere, with no hypocrisy or faking.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "If anyone can refute me, show me I'm making a mistake or looking at things from the wrong perspective, I'll gladly change. It's the truth I'm after, and the truth never harmed anyone.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Perfection of character is this: to live each day as if it were your last, without frenzy, without apathy, without pretence.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Today I escaped anxiety. Or no, I discarded it, because it was within me, in my own perceptions, not outside.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "It's time you realized that you have something in you more powerful and miraculous than the things that affect you and make you dance like a puppet.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Sprint for the finish. Write off your hopes, and if your well-being matters to you, be your own savior while you can.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "What is not good for the swarm is not good for the bee.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Let not your mind run on what you lack as much as on what you have already.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Men exist for the sake of one another. Teach them then or bear with them.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Short is the little which remains to thee of life. Live as on a mountain.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "To accept it without arrogance, to let it go with indifference.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Look beneath the surface; let not the several quality of a thing nor its worth escape thee.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Despise not death, but welcome it, for Nature wills it like all else.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Of human life the time is a point, and the substance is in a flux, and the perception dull.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Life is a warfare and a stranger's sojourn, and after-fame is oblivion.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "In a little while you will be nobody and nowhere, like Hadrian and Augustus.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Alexander the Great and his mule driver both died, and the same thing happened to both.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "When you run up against someone else's shamelessness, ask yourself: is a world without shameless people possible? No. Then don't ask the impossible.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "A cucumber is bitter. Throw it away. There are briars in the road. Turn aside from them. That is enough.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Don't let your imagination be crushed by life as a whole. Don't try to picture everything bad that could possibly happen.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "It never ceases to amaze me: we all love ourselves more than other people, but care more about their opinion than our own.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Remember that to change your mind and to follow him who corrects your error is as consistent with freedom as it is to persist in your error.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Whatever anyone does or says, I must be emerald and keep my colour.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "How much time he gains who does not look to see what his neighbour says or does or thinks.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "If you seek tranquility, do less. Or more accurately, do what's essential.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Ask yourself at every moment, 'Is this necessary?'", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "A limit of time is fixed for thee, which if thou dost not use for clearing away the clouds from thy mind, it will go and thou wilt go, and it will never return.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "No one can lose either the past or the future, for how can one be deprived of what one does not have?", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The longest-lived and the shortest-lived man, when they come to die, lose one and the same thing.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Everything harmonizes with me which is harmonious to thee, O Universe.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "All things are woven together and the common bond is sacred.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Constantly regard the universe as one living being, having one substance and one soul.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Love the art, poor as it may be, which thou hast learned, and be content with it.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Wipe out imagination; check desire; extinguish appetite; keep the ruling faculty in its own power.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Do not suppose that, because a thing is hard for you, it is impossible for anyone; but if something is possible for a man, consider it within your reach too.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Remember that all is opinion.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "When anything tempts you to feel bitter, remember: not 'This is a misfortune,' but 'To bear this worthily is good fortune.'", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "He who fears death either fears the loss of sensation or a different kind of sensation.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "A man then must stand erect, not be kept erect by others.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "If a man is mistaken, instruct him kindly and show him his error. But if you are not able, blame yourself, or blame not even yourself.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Observe always that everything is the result of change, and get used to thinking that there is nothing Nature loves so well as to change existing forms and to make new ones like them.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Your ability to control your thoughts: treat it with respect. It's all that protects your mind from false perceptions.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Don't be ashamed of needing help. Like a soldier storming a wall, you have a mission to accomplish. And if you've been wounded and you need a comrade to pull you up? So what?", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Concentrate every minute like a Roman, like a man, on doing what's in front of you with precise and genuine seriousness, tenderly, willingly, with justice.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "You could leave life right now. Let that determine what you do and say and think.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Learn to ask of all actions, 'Why are they doing that?' Starting with your own.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Do external things distract you? Then make time for yourself to learn something worthwhile; stop letting yourself be pulled in all directions.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "People who labor all their lives but have no purpose to direct every thought and impulse toward are wasting their time, even when hard at work.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Nothing happens to anyone that he can't endure.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Pass through this little space of time conformably to nature, and end thy journey in content, just as an olive falls off when it is ripe.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Asia and Europe are corners of the universe; all the sea a drop in the universe; Athos a little clod.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Remember: Matter. How tiny your share of it. Time. How brief and fleeting your allotment of it. Fate. How small a role you play in it.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "When jarred, unavoidably, by circumstances, revert at once to yourself, and don't lose the rhythm more than you can help.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "You don't have to turn this into something. It doesn't have to upset you.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Thou seest how few the things are, the which if a man lays hold of, he is able to live a life which flows in quiet.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "If you work at that which is before you, following right reason seriously, vigorously, calmly, without allowing anything else to distract you, you will live happy.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "We have come into being for cooperation, as have the feet, the hands, the eyelids, the rows of the upper and lower teeth.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "No man is tired of receiving what is useful. But it is useful to act according to nature. Do not then be tired of receiving what is useful by doing it to others.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "It's silly to try to escape other people's faults. They are inescapable. Just try to escape your own.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Labor not as one who is wretched, nor yet as one who would be pitied or admired.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "In your actions, don't procrastinate. In your conversations, don't confuse. In your thoughts, don't wander.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Don't be overheard complaining about life at court. Not even by yourself.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Have I done something for the general interest? Well then I have had my reward.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "When you've done well and another has benefited by it, why like a fool do you look for a third thing on top: credit for the good deed or a favor in return?", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "How easy it is to repel and wipe away every impression which is troublesome or unsuitable, and immediately to be in all tranquility.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "One thing only troubles me, lest I should do something which the constitution of man does not allow.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Pain is neither unbearable nor unending, as long as you keep in mind its limits and don't magnify it in your imagination.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Do not hope for Plato's Republic; but be content if the smallest thing goes on well.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Where a man can live, there he can also live well. But he must live in a palace; well then, he can also live well in a palace.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Do not be whirled about, but in every movement have respect to justice.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "The mind in itself wants nothing, unless it makes a want for itself.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "He who does wrong does wrong against himself. He who acts unjustly acts unjustly to himself, because he makes himself bad.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Often injustice lies in what you aren't doing, not only in what you are doing.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },
  { text: "Soon, very soon, thou wilt be ashes, or a skeleton, and either a name or not even a name.", author: "Marcus Aurelius", source: "Meditations" },

  // Seneca
  { text: "We suffer more often in imagination than in reality.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "It is not that we have a short time to live, but that we waste a lot of it.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "Life is long enough, and a sufficiently generous amount has been given to us for the highest achievements if it were all well invested.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "While we are postponing, life speeds by.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "As is a tale, so is life: not how long it is, but how good it is, is what matters.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Our plans miscarry because they have no aim. When a man does not know what harbour he is making for, no wind is the right wind.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Begin at once to live, and count each separate day as a separate life.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Wherever there is a human being, there is an opportunity for a kindness.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Happy Life" },
  { text: "No man was ever wise by chance.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Associate with those who will make a better man of you. Welcome those whom you yourself can improve. The process is mutual; for men learn while they teach.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "It is not the man who has too little, but the man who craves more, that is poor.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The primary indication of a well-ordered mind is a man's ability to remain in one place and linger in his own company.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Everywhere means nowhere.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Hold every hour in your grasp. Lay hold of today's task, and you will not need to depend so much upon tomorrow's.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Nothing, Lucilius, is ours, except time.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Set yourself free for your own sake; gather and save your time, which till lately has been forced from you, or filched away, or has merely slipped from your hands.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "What is quite unlooked for is more crushing in its effect, and unexpectedness adds to the weight of a disaster.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Let us prepare our minds as if we'd come to the very end of life. Let us postpone nothing. Let us balance life's books each day.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The whole future lies in uncertainty: live immediately.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "The greatest obstacle to living is expectancy, which hangs upon tomorrow and loses today.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "You live as if you were destined to live forever; no thought of your frailty ever enters your head.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "No one will bring back the years; no one will restore you to yourself.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "People are frugal in guarding their personal property; but as soon as it comes to squandering time they are most wasteful of the one thing in which it is right to be stingy.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "Of all people only those are at leisure who make time for philosophy, only those are really alive.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "It takes the whole of life to learn how to live, and, what will perhaps make you wonder more, it takes the whole of life to learn how to die.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "The part of life we really live is small. For all the rest of existence is not life, but merely time.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Shortness of Life" },
  { text: "The greatest remedy for anger is delay.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Anger" },
  { text: "Anger is like a falling rock which breaks itself to pieces upon the very thing which it crushes.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Anger" },
  { text: "Time discovers truth.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Anger" },
  { text: "Fire tests gold, suffering tests brave men.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Providence" },
  { text: "No man is more unhappy than he who never faces adversity. For he is not permitted to prove himself.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Providence" },
  { text: "Behold a spectacle worthy of the regard of God: a brave man matched with evil fortune.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Providence" },
  { text: "Disaster is virtue's opportunity.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Providence" },
  { text: "Sometimes even to live is an act of courage.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "It is a rough road that leads to the heights of greatness.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "If you really want to escape the things that harass you, what you need is not to be in a different place but to be a different person.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "You need a change of soul rather than a change of climate.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Your faults will follow you whithersoever you travel.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Until we have begun to go without them, we fail to realize how unnecessary many things are.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Set aside a certain number of days, during which you shall be content with the scantiest and cheapest fare, saying to yourself: 'Is this the condition that I feared?'", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "It is in times of security that the spirit should be preparing itself for difficult times.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The wise man regards riches as a slave, the fool as a master.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Happy Life" },
  { text: "Most powerful is he who has himself in his own power.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Philosophy teaches us to act, not to speak.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Philosophy moulds and constructs the soul; it orders our life, guides our conduct, shows us what we should do and what we should leave undone.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "He suffers more than necessary, who suffers before it is necessary.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "There is nothing the wise man does reluctantly.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "As long as you live, keep learning how to live.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Whatever can happen at any time can happen today.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Tranquility of Mind" },
  { text: "Nature has given us the seeds of knowledge, not knowledge itself.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Ponder for a long time whether you shall admit a given person to your friendship; but when you have decided to admit him, welcome him with all your heart and soul.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Friendship always benefits; love sometimes even injures.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "He who begins to be your friend because it pays will also cease because it pays.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Why do I make a friend? In order to have someone for whom I may die, whom I may follow into exile, against whose death I may stake my own life.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Most men ebb and flow in wretchedness between the fear of death and the hardships of life; they are unwilling to live, and yet they do not know how to die.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "No man enjoys the true taste of life but he who is willing and ready to quit it.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Life without the courage for death is slavery.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "He who has learned to die has unlearned slavery.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Show me a man who is not a slave; one is a slave to lust, another to greed, another to ambition, and all men are slaves to fear.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "'They are slaves,' people declare. Nay, rather they are men.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Treat your inferior as you would be treated by your superior.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "A great fortune is a great slavery.", author: "Seneca", source: "Consolation to Polybius" },
  { text: "You ask what is the proper limit to wealth? First, to have what is necessary; second, to have what is enough.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "If you live in harmony with nature you will never be poor; if you live according to what others think, you will never be rich.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The Fates lead the willing, and drag the unwilling.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Let us go to our sleep with joy and gladness; let us say: 'I have lived; the course which Fortune set for me is finished.'", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "If God is pleased to add another day, we should welcome it with glad hearts.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "That man is happiest, and is secure in his own possession of himself, who can await the morrow without apprehension.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "No good thing is pleasant to possess, without friends to share it.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The mind must be given relaxation; it will rise improved and sharper after a good break.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Tranquility of Mind" },
  { text: "We should take wandering outdoor walks, so that the mind might be nourished and refreshed by the open air and deep breathing.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Tranquility of Mind" },
  { text: "Real joy, believe me, is a stern matter.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Above all, my dear Lucilius, make this your business: learn how to feel joy.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The day which you fear as being the end of all things is the birthday of your eternity.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The time will come when diligent research over long periods will bring to light things which now lie hidden.", author: "Seneca", source: "Natural Questions" },
  { text: "Let us say what we feel, and feel what we say; let speech harmonize with life.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Just as the same chain fastens the prisoner and the soldier who guards him, so hope and fear, dissimilar as they are, keep step together.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "You must linger among a limited number of master thinkers, and digest their works, if you would derive ideas which shall win firm hold in your mind.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Each day acquire something which will fortify you against poverty, against death, indeed against other misfortunes as well.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "I feel, my dear Lucilius, that I am being not only reformed, but transformed.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Do you ask me what you should regard as especially to be avoided? I say, crowds.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "I come home more greedy, more ambitious, more voluptuous, and even more cruel and inhuman, because I have been among human beings.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "No man is crushed by misfortune unless he has first been deceived by prosperity.", author: "Seneca", source: "Consolation to Helvia" },
  { text: "It is not because things are difficult that we do not dare; it is because we do not dare that they are difficult.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Leisure without study is death; it is a tomb for the living man.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The best ideas are common property.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "What need is there to weep over parts of life? The whole of it calls for tears.", author: "Seneca", source: "Consolation to Marcia" },
  { text: "We are the parts of one great body. Nature produced us related to one another, since she created us from the same source and to the same end.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Our relations with one another are like a stone arch, which would collapse if the stones did not mutually support each other.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "God is near you, he is with you, he is within you.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Believe me, it is a great role: to play the role of one man.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The way is long if one follows precepts, but short and helpful, if one follows patterns.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "A man is as wretched as he has convinced himself that he is.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Pain is slight if opinion has added nothing to it.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Things that were hard to bear are sweet to remember.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Wild beasts flee the dangers they see, and once they have escaped them worry no more. We, however, are tormented alike by what is past and what is to come.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "I am not born for one corner; the whole world is my native land.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "No one is laughable who laughs at himself.", author: "Seneca", source: "On the Firmness of the Wise Man" },
  { text: "Men do not care how nobly they live, but only how long, although it is within the reach of every man to live nobly, but within no man's power to live long.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "The final hour when we cease to exist does not of itself bring death; it merely of itself completes the death-process.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Let us cherish and love old age; for it is full of pleasure if one knows how to use it.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "We learn our lessons, not for life, but for the lecture-room.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Hold fast to this sound and wholesome rule of life: that you indulge the body only so far as is needful for good health.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "It does not matter how many books you have, but how good they are.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "It is the mind that makes us rich.", author: "Seneca", source: "Consolation to Helvia" },
  { text: "Certain moments are torn from us, some are gently removed, and others glide beyond our reach.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "What man can you show me who places any value on his time, who reckons the worth of each day, who understands that he is dying daily?", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "He robs present ills of their power who has perceived their coming beforehand.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Virtue is nothing else than right reason.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "A pilot can still sail even when his canvas is rent.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "He who fears death will never do anything worthy of a man who is alive.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Tranquility of Mind" },
  { text: "Happy is the man who can make others better, not merely when he is in their company, but even when he is in their thoughts!", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Cherish some man of high character, and keep him ever before your eyes, living as if he were watching you, and ordering all your actions as if he beheld them.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Without a ruler to do it by, you can't make crooked straight.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Nature does not bestow virtue; to become good is an art.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Cling tooth and nail to the rule: not to give in to adversity, never to trust prosperity, and always take full note of fortune's habit of behaving just as she pleases.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "No man is free who is a slave to his body.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "Fortune can take away riches, but not courage.", author: "Seneca", source: "Medea" },
  { text: "A good conscience welcomes the crowd, but a bad conscience is anxious and troubled even in solitude.", author: "Seneca", source: "Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "No evil can befall a good man.", author: "Seneca", source: "On Providence" },

  // Epictetus
  { text: "Some things are within our power, while others are not. Within our power are opinion, motivation, desire, aversion, and, in a word, whatever is of our own doing.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "The things within our power are by nature free, unrestrained, unhindered; but those beyond our power are weak, slavish, restrained, belonging to others.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Practice, then, from the start to say to every harsh impression, 'You are an impression, and not at all the thing you appear to be.'", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Men are disturbed not by things, but by the views which they take of things.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Death is nothing terrible, else it would have appeared so to Socrates. But the terror consists in our notion of death, that it is terrible.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "An uninstructed person will lay the fault of his own bad condition upon others. One just starting instruction will lay it on himself. One perfectly instructed will place blame neither on others nor on himself.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Don't demand that things happen as you wish, but wish that they happen as they do happen, and you will go on well.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Sickness is a hindrance to the body, but not to your ability to choose, unless that is your choice.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Never say of anything, 'I have lost it'; but, 'I have returned it.'", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "If you want to improve, be content to be thought foolish and stupid with regard to external things.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Remember that you must behave in life as at a dinner party.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Remember that you are an actor in a drama, of such a kind as the author pleases to make it. Your business is to act well the character assigned you; to choose it is another's.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "You may be unconquerable, if you enter into no combat in which it is not in your own control to conquer.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "It is not he who reviles you or strikes you who insults you, but it is your opinion about these things as being insulting.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Let death and exile, and all other things which appear terrible, be daily before your eyes, but chiefly death, and you will never entertain any abject thought.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "If you wish to be a philosopher, prepare yourself from the very first to be laughed at.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Everything has two handles: one by which it may be carried, the other by which it cannot.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "If anyone tells you that a certain person speaks ill of you, do not make excuses but answer: 'He was ignorant of my other faults, else he would not have mentioned these alone.'", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Let silence be your general rule; or say only what is necessary and in few words.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "First say to yourself what you would be; and then do what you have to do.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Never call yourself a philosopher, nor talk a great deal among the unlearned about theorems, but act conformably to them.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Sheep don't throw up the grass to show the shepherds how much they have eaten; but, inwardly digesting their food, they outwardly produce wool and milk.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Whatever rules you have deliberately proposed to yourself, abide by them as laws.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "How long are you going to wait before you demand the best for yourself?", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Even if you are not yet a Socrates, you ought to live as one who wishes to be a Socrates.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "If you kiss your child or wife, say that you only kiss things which are human, and thus you will not be disturbed if either of them dies.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Be not elated at any excellence which is not your own.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Freedom is not procured by a full enjoyment of what is desired, but by controlling the desire.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "No man is free who is not master of himself.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "Only the educated are free.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "It is impossible for a man to learn what he thinks he already knows.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "It is difficulties that show what men are.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "If a person gave your body to any passerby, you would be furious. Yet you hand over your mind to anyone who comes along, to be disturbed and confused. Are you not ashamed of that?", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "He is a wise man who does not grieve for the things which he has not, but rejoices for those which he has.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "Nothing great is created suddenly, any more than a bunch of grapes or a fig. If you tell me that you desire a fig, I answer that there must be time.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "What, then, is to be done? To make the best of what is in our power, and take the rest as it naturally happens.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "It is not death or pain that is to be dreaded, but the fear of pain or death.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "If you would be a good reader, read; if a writer, write.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Every habit and faculty is preserved and increased by its corresponding actions.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "If you wish not to be of an angry temper, do not feed the habit; give it nothing to help its increase.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Count the days on which you have not been angry. I used to be angry every day; now every other day; then every third and fourth day.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Who then is invincible? It is he whom none of the things disturb which are independent of the will.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "I must die. Must I then die lamenting? I must be put in chains. Must I then also lament?", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "You may fetter my leg, but my will not even Zeus himself can overpower.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "A good man does nothing for appearance's sake, but for the sake of having done right.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Practice yourself, for heaven's sake, in little things; and thence proceed to greater.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "The beginning of philosophy is a consciousness of your own weakness and inability in necessary things.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Bear and forbear.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "He is free who lives as he wishes to live; who is neither subject to compulsion nor to hindrance, nor to force.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "A ship should not ride on a single anchor, nor life on a single hope.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "Fortify yourself with contentment, for this is an impregnable fortress.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "What is the first business of one who practices philosophy? To get rid of self-conceit.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "It is we who torment, we who make difficulties for ourselves; that is, our opinions do.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "When I see a man in a state of anxiety, I say, 'What can this man want?'", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "What would have become of Hercules, do you think, if there had been no lion, hydra, stag or boar?", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Whoever then wishes to be free, let him neither wish for anything nor avoid anything which depends on others.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "If your companion be dirty, he who keeps company with him must of necessity get dirty too, even though he himself happens to be clean.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Consider at what price you sell your integrity; but please, for God's sake, don't sell it cheap.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Know, first, who you are, and then adorn yourself accordingly.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Imagine for yourself a character, a model personality, whose example you determine to follow, in private as well as in public.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "You are a little soul carrying around a corpse.", author: "Epictetus", source: "quoted in Meditations" },
  { text: "Is there smoke in the house? If it is moderate, I will stay; if excessive, I go out.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Another person will not harm you unless you wish it; but you will be harmed when you think you are being harmed.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Our duties are universally measured by our relations.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "If you have assumed a character beyond your strength, you have both demeaned yourself in that, and quitted one which you might have supported.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "As in walking you take care not to tread upon a nail, or turn your foot, so likewise take care not to hurt the ruling faculty of your mind.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "The body is to everyone the measure of the possessions proper for it, as the foot is of the shoe.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "These reasonings are unconnected: 'I am richer than you, therefore I am better'; 'I am more eloquent than you, therefore I am better.'", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Does anyone bathe in a hurry? Don't say that he does it ill, but in a hurry.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Upon every accident, remember to turn toward yourself and inquire what faculty you have for its use.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "It is better to die with hunger, exempt from grief and fear, than to live in affluence with perturbation.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Begin therefore with little things. Is a little oil spilt? A little wine stolen? Say to yourself, 'This is the price paid for tranquility, and nothing is to be had for nothing.'", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Zeus has entrusted me with myself.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Philosophy does not promise to secure anything external for man.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "As wood is the material of the carpenter, and bronze of the sculptor, so each man's own life is the material of the art of living.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "No one who is a lover of money, a lover of pleasure, or a lover of glory, is likewise a lover of mankind; but only he who is a lover of virtue.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "If you wish to be good, first believe that you are bad.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "It is more necessary for the soul to be cured than the body; for it is better to die than to live badly.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "Whenever you grow attached to something, do not act as though it were one of those things that cannot be taken away, but as though it were something like a jar or a crystal goblet.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "You will do the greatest service to the state if you shall raise, not the roofs of the houses, but the souls of the citizens.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "You are not flesh nor hair, but you are will; if you have this beautiful, then you will be beautiful.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "If anyone is unhappy, let him remember that he is unhappy by reason of himself alone.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "In prosperity it is very easy to find a friend; in adversity, nothing is so difficult.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "When any person does ill by you, or speaks ill of you, remember that he acts or speaks from a supposition of its being his duty.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Enchiridion" },
  { text: "What is the fruit of these doctrines? Precisely the most noble and the most becoming to those who are truly educated: tranquility, fearlessness, and freedom.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "To a reasonable creature, that alone is unsupportable which is unreasonable; but everything reasonable may be supported.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Seek not good from without: seek it within yourselves, or you will never find it.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Where is the good? In the will. Where is the evil? In the will. Where is neither of them? In those things which are independent of the will.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "What is it that disturbs and terrifies the multitude? Is it the tyrant and his guards? No; it is their opinions.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },
  { text: "Let others practise lawsuits, others study problems, others syllogisms; here you practise how to die, how to be enchained, how to be racked, how to be exiled.", author: "Epictetus", source: "Discourses" },

  // Musonius Rufus
  { text: "If you accomplish something good with hard work, the labor passes quickly, but the good endures; if you do something shameful in pursuit of pleasure, the pleasure passes quickly, but the shame endures.", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "We will train both soul and body when we accustom ourselves to cold, heat, thirst, hunger, scarcity of food, hardness of bed, and abstaining from pleasures.", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Lectures" },
  { text: "All of us are naturally disposed to live without error and nobly.", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Lectures" },
  { text: "To relax the mind is to lose it.", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "We begin to lose our hesitation to do immoral things when we lose our hesitation to speak of them.", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Fragments" },
  { text: "Virtue is not simply theoretical knowledge, but it is practical application as well.", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Lectures" },
  { text: "Women as well as men have received from the gods the gift of reason.", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Lectures" },
  { text: "Is not the world the common country of all men?", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Lectures" },
  { text: "The beginning and foundation of self-control lies in self-control in eating and drinking.", author: "Musonius Rufus", source: "Lectures" },

  // Zeno of Citium
  { text: "Well-being is attained little by little, and nevertheless is no little thing itself.", author: "Zeno of Citium", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },
  { text: "We have two ears and one mouth, so we should listen more than we say.", author: "Zeno of Citium", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },
  { text: "The goal of life is living in agreement with nature.", author: "Zeno of Citium", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },
  { text: "A friend is another self.", author: "Zeno of Citium", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },
  { text: "Better to trip with the feet than with the tongue.", author: "Zeno of Citium", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },
  { text: "Nothing is more hostile to a firm grasp on knowledge than self-deception.", author: "Zeno of Citium", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },
  { text: "I made a prosperous voyage when I suffered shipwreck.", author: "Zeno of Citium", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },

  // Cleanthes
  { text: "Lead me, O Zeus, and thou my Destiny, to that goal long ago to me assigned. I'll follow and not falter; if my will prove weak and craven, still I'll follow.", author: "Cleanthes", source: "Hymn, quoted in Enchiridion" },
  { text: "Nothing is done on earth apart from thee, O God, nor in the heavens nor in the sea, save what the wicked do in their own folly.", author: "Cleanthes", source: "Hymn to Zeus" },

  // Chrysippus
  { text: "If I had followed the multitude, I should not have studied philosophy.", author: "Chrysippus", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },
  { text: "Give me the doctrines; I'll find the proofs myself.", author: "Chrysippus", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },
  { text: "Living virtuously is equal to living in accordance with one's experience of the actual course of nature.", author: "Chrysippus", source: "Diogenes Laertius, Lives" },

  // Cato the Younger
  { text: "I begin to speak only when I am certain what I shall say is not better left unsaid.", author: "Cato the Younger", source: "Plutarch, Life of Cato" },
  { text: "Now I am master of myself.", author: "Cato the Younger", source: "Plutarch, Life of Cato" },

  // Hecato
  { text: "Cease to hope and you will cease to fear.", author: "Hecato", source: "quoted in Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "What progress have I made? I have begun to be a friend to myself.", author: "Hecato", source: "quoted in Letters to Lucilius" },
  { text: "I can show you a love potion without drugs, herbs, or any witch's spell: if you wish to be loved, love.", author: "Hecato", source: "quoted in Letters to Lucilius" },

  // Cicero, on Stoic doctrine
  { text: "That which is honourable is the only good.", author: "Cicero", source: "Paradoxa Stoicorum" },
  { text: "The wise man alone is free, and every fool is a slave.", author: "Cicero", source: "Paradoxa Stoicorum" },
  { text: "True law is right reason in agreement with nature.", author: "Cicero", source: "On the Republic" },
];
